1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,720
Welcome to Rashi in Brief, where each week

2
00:00:02,800 --> 00:00:05,126
we explore the Torah portion through the eyes

3
00:00:05,153 --> 00:00:09,206
of the great medieval commentator, Rabbi Shlomo Yitzchaki,

4
00:00:09,273 --> 00:00:10,886
known as Rashi.

5
00:00:11,030 --> 00:00:13,526
This week we're looking at the special reading

6
00:00:13,586 --> 00:00:16,486
for Rosh Hashana, the Jewish New Year, which

7
00:00:16,539 --> 00:00:19,446
is taken from Genesis chapter twenty-one, and it

8
00:00:19,526 --> 00:00:22,806
is packed with dramatic moments that Rashi brings

9
00:00:22,833 --> 00:00:27,531
to life with his signature close reading.This portion

10
00:00:27,584 --> 00:00:29,211
tells the story of one of the most

11
00:00:29,284 --> 00:00:32,652
anticipated events in the Torah: the birth of

12
00:00:32,759 --> 00:00:36,172
Isaac to Abraham and Sarah, after decades of

13
00:00:36,222 --> 00:00:36,892
waiting.

14
00:00:37,079 --> 00:00:40,572
Sarah, who had been barren her entire life,

15
00:00:40,642 --> 00:00:43,852
finally gives birth in her old age, just

16
00:00:43,932 --> 00:00:45,452
as God had promised.

17
00:00:45,602 --> 00:00:48,572
Abraham makes a great feast when the child

18
00:00:48,625 --> 00:00:49,452
is weaned.

19
00:00:49,572 --> 00:00:52,492
But joy quickly gives way to tension.

20
00:00:52,652 --> 00:00:56,092
Sarah sees Ishmael, the son Abraham had with

21
00:00:56,132 --> 00:00:59,692
Hagar, behaving in a way she finds threatening,

22
00:00:59,712 --> 00:01:02,412
and she demands that Abraham send Hagar and

23
00:01:02,492 --> 00:01:03,852
Ishmael away.

24
00:01:04,012 --> 00:01:07,852
Abraham is deeply distressed, but God tells him

25
00:01:07,879 --> 00:01:09,372
to listen to Sarah.

26
00:01:09,652 --> 00:01:13,212
Hagar and Ishmael wander in the wilderness, nearly

27
00:01:13,279 --> 00:01:16,252
dying of thirst before God opens their eyes

28
00:01:16,305 --> 00:01:17,132
to a well.

29
00:01:17,372 --> 00:01:20,812
The portion closes with Abraham making a treaty

30
00:01:20,844 --> 00:01:22,972
with Abimelech at Be'er Sheva.

31
00:01:23,265 --> 00:01:27,772
Rashi, as always, zeroes in on unusual words,

32
00:01:27,823 --> 00:01:31,372
structural puzzles, and moral complexities that we might

33
00:01:31,436 --> 00:01:36,280
otherwise glide right past.Let's start at the very

34
00:01:36,320 --> 00:01:37,479
beginning of the chapter.

35
00:01:37,679 --> 00:01:42,282
The Torah says, "And the Lord visited Sarah,"

36
00:01:42,301 --> 00:01:44,282
and this comes right after the story of

37
00:01:44,362 --> 00:01:47,402
Abraham praying for Abimelech's household to be healed

38
00:01:47,455 --> 00:01:48,521
of infertility.

39
00:01:48,922 --> 00:01:53,241
Rashi asks a structural question: why are these

40
00:01:53,301 --> 00:01:55,482
two stories placed side by side?

41
00:01:55,762 --> 00:01:59,402
His answer is a powerful spiritual principle.

42
00:01:59,594 --> 00:02:02,602
When a person prays on behalf of another

43
00:02:02,661 --> 00:02:05,961
for something that he himself also needs, the

44
00:02:06,021 --> 00:02:08,602
one who prays is answered first.

45
00:02:08,792 --> 00:02:12,841
Abraham prayed for Abimelech's wife to bear children,

46
00:02:12,902 --> 00:02:16,122
and Abraham himself needed that same blessing for

47
00:02:16,215 --> 00:02:16,762
Sarah.

48
00:02:17,108 --> 00:02:20,841
So God remembered Sarah even before He healed

49
00:02:20,894 --> 00:02:21,642
Abimelech.

50
00:02:21,862 --> 00:02:25,802
The placement of these passages isn't accidental; it's

51
00:02:25,822 --> 00:02:28,284
the Torah's way of teaching us that generosity

52
00:02:28,337 --> 00:02:32,181
in prayer is rewarded.Next, let's look at one

53
00:02:32,208 --> 00:02:34,661
of Rashi's most striking comments.

54
00:02:34,773 --> 00:02:37,381
When Isaac is weaned and Abraham throws a

55
00:02:37,448 --> 00:02:40,185
feast, the Torah describes it as a "mishteh

56
00:02:40,265 --> 00:02:42,745
gadol," a great feast.

57
00:02:42,841 --> 00:02:44,425
What makes it great?

58
00:02:44,718 --> 00:02:47,785
Rashi says it's called great because the great

59
00:02:47,854 --> 00:02:51,545
people of the generation were present: Shem, Eber,

60
00:02:51,605 --> 00:02:52,745
and Abimelech.

61
00:02:52,885 --> 00:02:55,945
The word "great" doesn't describe the quantity of

62
00:02:56,041 --> 00:02:58,665
food or the lavishness of the celebration.

63
00:02:58,745 --> 00:03:01,385
It describes the stature of the guests.

64
00:03:01,529 --> 00:03:05,625
This is classic Rashi, taking a seemingly simple

65
00:03:05,689 --> 00:03:08,905
adjective and showing us that it carries specific,

66
00:03:08,963 --> 00:03:12,962
meaningful information about the event.Now we come to

67
00:03:13,002 --> 00:03:15,362
the most dramatic and troubling moment in the

68
00:03:15,432 --> 00:03:16,162
portion.

69
00:03:16,309 --> 00:03:20,722
Sarah sees Ishmael "metzachek," a word usually translated

70
00:03:20,802 --> 00:03:25,042
as "laughing" or "playing." But Rashi insists this

71
00:03:25,095 --> 00:03:26,722
is no innocent play.

72
00:03:26,935 --> 00:03:30,482
He brings three interpretations from the Midrash: the

73
00:03:30,530 --> 00:03:33,042
word can refer to idol worship, as it

74
00:03:33,090 --> 00:03:36,162
does when describing the Golden Calf; it can

75
00:03:36,215 --> 00:03:38,802
refer to sexual immorality, as it does in

76
00:03:38,822 --> 00:03:41,602
the story of Potiphar's wife; or it can

77
00:03:41,669 --> 00:03:43,762
refer to murder, as it does in the

78
00:03:43,829 --> 00:03:47,122
story of Abner's men who "played" before killing

79
00:03:47,170 --> 00:03:47,922
each other.

80
00:03:48,215 --> 00:03:51,122
Rashi then adds that Ishmael was quarreling with

81
00:03:51,215 --> 00:03:54,562
Isaac over the inheritance, claiming the double portion

82
00:03:54,615 --> 00:03:57,042
of a firstborn, and that he took Isaac

83
00:03:57,102 --> 00:03:59,442
out into the field and shot arrows at

84
00:03:59,502 --> 00:04:01,762
him, pretending it was just a game.

85
00:04:01,938 --> 00:04:05,362
This is why Sarah reacts so fiercely.

86
00:04:05,522 --> 00:04:08,162
She isn't being petty or jealous.

87
00:04:08,262 --> 00:04:10,482
She sees a genuine threat to her son's

88
00:04:10,562 --> 00:04:12,402
life and spiritual future.

89
00:04:12,562 --> 00:04:14,962
And when she says, "The son of this

90
00:04:15,026 --> 00:04:17,762
bondwoman shall not inherit with my son, with

91
00:04:17,869 --> 00:04:22,562
Isaac," Rashi unpacks her precise language: even if

92
00:04:22,589 --> 00:04:24,962
he weren't Isaac, as my son he would

93
00:04:25,012 --> 00:04:28,082
deserve the inheritance over Ishmael; and even if

94
00:04:28,135 --> 00:04:30,482
he weren't my son, as the righteous man

95
00:04:30,562 --> 00:04:33,322
Isaac is, he would still deserve it.

96
00:04:33,432 --> 00:04:35,682
How much more so when both qualities are

97
00:04:35,762 --> 00:04:36,642
combined.

98
00:04:36,789 --> 00:04:39,282
Sarah's words are not redundant.

99
00:04:39,346 --> 00:04:42,242
They are a carefully constructed legal and moral

100
00:04:42,322 --> 00:04:47,202
argument.When Abraham is distressed about sending Ishmael away,

101
00:04:47,262 --> 00:04:51,042
God tells him, "Shema b'kolah," listen to her

102
00:04:51,135 --> 00:04:51,842
voice.

103
00:04:52,242 --> 00:04:56,162
Rashi draws a remarkable conclusion from this phrase:

104
00:04:56,302 --> 00:05:00,002
Abraham was inferior to Sarah in prophecy.

105
00:05:00,222 --> 00:05:03,922
God is essentially telling Abraham that Sarah's spiritual

106
00:05:03,980 --> 00:05:06,642
perception in this matter is more accurate than

107
00:05:06,682 --> 00:05:07,442
his own.

108
00:05:07,618 --> 00:05:11,682
This is a striking comment, elevating Sarah's prophetic

109
00:05:11,746 --> 00:05:15,202
authority and reminding us that moral clarity sometimes

110
00:05:15,269 --> 00:05:20,953
comes from unexpected directions.Perhaps the most theologically profound

111
00:05:21,013 --> 00:05:23,993
comment Rashi makes in this portion concerns the

112
00:05:24,062 --> 00:05:26,473
moment when Ishmael is dying of thirst in

113
00:05:26,493 --> 00:05:27,273
the wilderness.

114
00:05:27,473 --> 00:05:31,194
The Torah says God heard "the voice of

115
00:05:31,214 --> 00:05:35,034
the lad where he is." Rashi explains that

116
00:05:35,074 --> 00:05:37,994
the phrase "where he is" — "ba'asher hu

117
00:05:38,090 --> 00:05:41,274
sham" — means God judges a person according

118
00:05:41,327 --> 00:05:44,074
to their present actions, not according to what

119
00:05:44,122 --> 00:05:45,514
they may do in the future.

120
00:05:45,794 --> 00:05:50,154
The angels protested, arguing that Ishmael's descendants would

121
00:05:50,214 --> 00:05:52,474
one day kill Israelites with thirst.

122
00:05:52,694 --> 00:05:57,114
But God asked a single question: right now,

123
00:05:57,194 --> 00:05:59,034
is he righteous or wicked?

124
00:05:59,294 --> 00:06:02,474
The angels answered: righteous.

125
00:06:02,714 --> 00:06:06,074
And God said, then I will judge him

126
00:06:06,154 --> 00:06:07,754
as he is right now.

127
00:06:08,287 --> 00:06:11,914
Rashi goes on to tell the devastating story

128
00:06:11,967 --> 00:06:15,434
of how Ishmael's descendants would later offer inflated,

129
00:06:15,487 --> 00:06:19,434
empty waterskins to thirsty Israelite exiles, who died

130
00:06:19,466 --> 00:06:21,994
when they opened them and swallowed only air.

131
00:06:22,294 --> 00:06:26,074
Yet despite this terrible future, God saved the

132
00:06:26,174 --> 00:06:27,834
boy in the present moment.

133
00:06:28,074 --> 00:06:33,114
This comment teaches something profound about divine justice:

134
00:06:33,194 --> 00:06:35,514
mercy is extended based on who a person

135
00:06:35,647 --> 00:06:38,074
is in this moment, not who they might

136
00:06:38,154 --> 00:06:38,714
become.

137
00:06:39,034 --> 00:06:42,634
For Rosh Hashana, a day of judgment, this

138
00:06:42,674 --> 00:06:46,131
could not be more relevant.Finally, at the end

139
00:06:46,184 --> 00:06:49,011
of the portion, Abraham plants an "eshel" at

140
00:06:49,064 --> 00:06:49,971
Be'er Sheva.

141
00:06:50,238 --> 00:06:53,971
Rashi brings a disagreement between two great sages,

142
00:06:54,071 --> 00:06:56,771
Rav and Shmuel: one says it was an

143
00:06:56,819 --> 00:06:59,091
orchard to provide fruit for guests, and the

144
00:06:59,131 --> 00:07:01,171
other says it was an inn stocked with

145
00:07:01,211 --> 00:07:02,771
all kinds of provisions.

146
00:07:02,911 --> 00:07:06,051
But either way, the purpose was hospitality.

147
00:07:06,211 --> 00:07:09,411
And Rashi adds that through this eshel, Abraham

148
00:07:09,451 --> 00:07:11,171
would call out the name of God to

149
00:07:11,251 --> 00:07:12,291
all who came.

150
00:07:12,424 --> 00:07:15,011
After his guests ate and drank, he would

151
00:07:15,059 --> 00:07:17,491
tell them: don't thank me, thank the One

152
00:07:17,544 --> 00:07:19,891
whose food you have actually eaten, the One

153
00:07:19,931 --> 00:07:22,371
who spoke and the universe came into being.

154
00:07:22,511 --> 00:07:26,051
Abraham's hospitality wasn't just kindness.

155
00:07:26,184 --> 00:07:28,291
It was a vehicle for spreading awareness of

156
00:07:28,391 --> 00:07:32,937
God throughout the world.Stepping back, we can see

157
00:07:32,977 --> 00:07:35,817
several themes running through Rashi's commentary on this

158
00:07:35,887 --> 00:07:36,617
portion.

159
00:07:36,697 --> 00:07:39,097
There is the theme of divine promise and

160
00:07:39,157 --> 00:07:42,297
fulfillment: God said Sarah would bear a son,

161
00:07:42,337 --> 00:07:44,857
and every detail of that promise was kept,

162
00:07:44,905 --> 00:07:46,617
down to the exact timing.

163
00:07:46,697 --> 00:07:49,737
There is the theme of justice and judgment:

164
00:07:49,797 --> 00:07:52,937
who deserves the inheritance, how God evaluates a

165
00:07:52,994 --> 00:07:55,897
person in the present moment, and what constitutes

166
00:07:55,967 --> 00:07:58,697
genuine threat versus apparent play.

167
00:07:58,837 --> 00:08:01,977
And there is the theme of spiritual perception:

168
00:08:02,044 --> 00:08:05,817
Sarah's prophetic insight, Abraham's generous prayer, and the

169
00:08:05,877 --> 00:08:08,537
way hospitality becomes a form of teaching about

170
00:08:08,637 --> 00:08:09,097
God.

171
00:08:09,310 --> 00:08:11,737
Rashi shows us that every word in the

172
00:08:11,777 --> 00:08:15,338
Torah is doing work, that nothing is accidental,

173
00:08:15,378 --> 00:08:17,338
and that beneath the surface of these ancient

174
00:08:17,418 --> 00:08:20,458
stories lie principles that speak directly to how

175
00:08:20,511 --> 00:08:24,858
we understand mercy, justice, and faithfulness, themes that

176
00:08:24,898 --> 00:08:28,876
are at the very heart of Rosh Hashana.Thank

177
00:08:28,936 --> 00:08:31,436
you so much for joining me on Rashi

178
00:08:31,516 --> 00:08:32,156
in Brief.

179
00:08:32,436 --> 00:08:35,036
Wishing all of you a shanah tovah, a

180
00:08:35,116 --> 00:08:37,676
good and sweet new year, and I look

181
00:08:37,716 --> 00:08:40,156
forward to learning with you again next week.

